{
  "id": "COMPOUND:21164 - PROTEIN:6256",
  "verbalization": "Isonaamine C appears to exert its biological effect through a series of interconnected molecular pathways, ultimately targeting Histone H1.0. The evidence suggests that Isonaamine C acts as a common target for multiple compounds, all of which are involved in the regulation of histone modification. Specifically, Isonaamine C is linked to RWLeu4, which in turn is a target of three different compounds—beta-D-Galactopyranuronic acid, E-Omega-Hydroxyferulenol, and Methyl P-Hydroxycinnamate—all of which ultimately target Histone H1.0. This indicates that Isonaamine C may influence histone function through a shared mechanism, possibly by modulating the activity of these intermediates. The presence of multiple distinct compounds targeting the same histone suggests a potential role in histone post-translational modifications, which are critical for chromatin structure and gene regulation. The integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Isonaamine C and Histone H1.0, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
